<p>The jump from 16 to 18 is smaller than the jump from 14 to 16 — most of the explosive natural development has already occurred by 16, which is exactly why training quality starts mattering more than age alone from this point forward. A 16-year-old who trains seriously can realistically close the gap to where an untrained 18-year-old sits, or even surpass it, purely through consistent programming.</p>
