<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is the average vertical jump for a 16 year old boy?</div>
<div class="faq-a">The average vertical jump for a 16 year old boy is approximately 16 to 20 inches. Boys who play varsity sports average 20 to 25 inches. Above 25 inches is considered excellent at this age, and above 28 inches starts to attract college recruiting attention for skill positions. Use our <a href="/vertical-jump-percentile">Vertical Jump Calculator</a> with the teen age group selected to get your exact percentile.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Is a 28 inch vertical good for a 16 year old?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Yes — a 28-inch vertical is well above average and considered excellent for a 16-year-old male, placing him in approximately the 90th to 92nd percentile for his age. For a 16-year-old female, 28 inches would be far beyond elite norms for that age group. A 28-inch vertical at 16 is the kind of number that starts appearing on college recruiting profiles for basketball, football skill positions, and track.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Can a 16 year old dunk a basketball?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Yes, dunking is realistic for many athletic 16 year olds, particularly those with good height or a strong vertical jump relative to their height. A 6-foot-tall 16-year-old needs roughly a 24 to 26 inch vertical to dunk — solidly within the "good" range for this age. Shorter athletes with elite verticals (28 inches and above) can also dunk. Use our <a href="/can-i-dunk-calculator">Can I Dunk calculator</a> to check your specific situation by height and vertical.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How fast will my vertical jump improve at 16?</div>
<div class="faq-a">At 16, athletes can still see significant improvements with focused training, though gains come more from deliberate work than from natural growth compared to 14. A committed 8-week program combining squats and plyometrics can add 3 to 6 inches at this age. Continued training through senior year can realistically add 8 to 12 inches of total development by graduation, which is meaningful for both performance and recruiting purposes.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What vertical jump should a 16 year old aim for to get recruited?</div>
<div class="faq-a">For a 16-year-old male basketball or football skill position prospect, aiming for 26 to 30 inches by junior year is a realistic and competitive target for serious recruiting attention. This would put you in the top 10 to 15 percent of athletes your age. For female athletes, 20 to 24 inches by 16 is competitive for college-level basketball and volleyball recruiting. Remember that vertical jump is one data point among many — film, skill, and overall production matter more, but a strong number gives recruiters a reason to take a closer look.</div>
</div>
